>
  <Fragment slot="exact">
    <h2>1. Acceptance and scope</h2>
    <p>
      These terms apply to use of the public FairMind website and its published materials. Access
      to a FairMind product workspace may be governed by a separate agreement. If you do not agree
      to these terms, do not use the website.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Informational and research material</h2>
    <p>
      Website content describes product capabilities, implementation states, research methods, and
      known limitations. Research-stage, metadata-only, external-provider, unavailable, and
      validated paths are not interchangeable. Nothing on the website is a guarantee that an AI
      system is safe, lawful, compliant, certified, or suitable for release.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Human decision boundary</h2>
    <p>
      Evaluation outputs, findings, evidence mappings, and readiness signals support review. They
      do not replace professional judgment or an accountable decision-maker. You remain responsible
      for deciding how to use outputs in your technical, legal, security, and governance context.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Acceptable use</h2>
    <p>
      Do not interfere with the website, attempt unauthorized access, abuse access-request forms,
      introduce malicious code, scrape in a manner that degrades service, or use published material
      to misrepresent a certification, endorsement, or regulatory conclusion.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Intellectual property and open source</h2>
    <p>
      Website design, copy, and branding are protected by applicable rights. Code and research
      artifacts published in the FairMind repository are governed by the licenses and notices in
      that repository. A public link does not remove those terms.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Availability and changes</h2>
    <p>
      FairMind may change or discontinue website content and may update these terms. The current
      version and date appear on this page. We do not promise uninterrupted availability or that
      every described capability is deployed in every environment.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Disclaimers</h2>
    <p>
      The public website and its content are provided on an “as available” basis to the extent
      permitted by law. FairMind disclaims implied warranties that cannot be supported by the
      evidence and scope described on the page.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="plain">
    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <p>
      The website explains what FairMind is building and what the current evidence supports. It is
      not a certificate generator, legal opinion, or automatic permission to ship an AI system.
    </p>
    <h2>Use the labels</h2>
    <p>
      “Validated path,” “active research,” “metadata only,” and “unavailable” describe materially
      different capability states. Please do not flatten them into “FairMind verified this.”
    </p>
    <h2>Keep a person accountable</h2>
    <p>
      Use technical results as evidence. Review their limitations. Then let the responsible person
      make and document the decision.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="path">
    <h2>Responsibility path</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>FairMind supplies</th><th>You remain responsible for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Evaluation</td><td>Scoped result, provenance, and limitations</td><td>Correct system definition and appropriate test choice</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Finding</td><td>Evidence-linked issue and suggested next work</td><td>Impact analysis and remediation decision</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Retest</td><td>Linked current result with retained history</td><td>Confirming the change addresses the real risk</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Governance</td><td>Review packet, open gaps, and rationale context</td><td>The final release or operating decision</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
